WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.937
Before changing dtypes or encoding categories, inspect the dataframe.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:09.362
Head shows sample rows. Info reports current technical dtypes and non-null counts.

00:00:09.362 --> 00:00:12.052
Missingness counts reveal completeness issues.

00:00:12.052 --> 00:00:16.447
Unique-value counts can help identify categories or suspicious identifiers.

00:00:16.447 --> 00:00:21.107
These commands provide evidence. They do not decide the analytical meaning for us.

00:00:21.107 --> 00:00:25.702
We combine the software output with our understanding of what each variable represents.

00:00:25.702 --> 00:00:31.857
Connect this idea to a hospital combining patient tables, device JSON, and clinical notes.

00:00:31.857 --> 00:00:36.477
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.477 --> 00:00:43.012
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.012 --> 00:00:47.647
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:52.480
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
